---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import SocialTool from '../components/SocialTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize an Image for Instagram, No Upload | SizeTune';
const description =
  "Fit a photo to Instagram's feed and Reel cover sizes in your browser, with crop, colour bars or a blurred background. Sizes come from Instagram's Help Center.";
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize an image for Instagram', '/instagram-image-resizer/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/instagram-image-resizer/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize an image for Instagram" lead="Pick a size, choose how the picture should fit, check the preview and download a JPEG. Every size on this page comes from Instagram's own Help Center, and nothing is uploaded." />

  <SocialTool client:load platform="instagram" />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool fits images inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to use it. The notes below on Instagram's photo sizes still apply with any other editor.
    </p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-base text-slate-700">
    Need a file under a size limit instead? <a data-testid="to-resizer" class="font-medium text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4" href="/">Open the KB resizer</a>.
  </p>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What Instagram says about photo sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Instagram's Help Center says photos are shared at the best resolution possible, up to a width of 1,080 pixels. A
      photo between 320 and 1,080 pixels wide keeps its resolution as long as its shape sits between 1.91:1 (wide) and
      3:4 (tall). That is a height between 566 and 1,440 pixels at the full 1,080 width. A photo outside that range is
      cropped to fit, and a larger photo is scaled down to 1,080 pixels wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sizes in the tool follow those numbers: a 1,080 by 1,080 square, a 1,080 by 1,440 portrait at the tallest
      shape the page lists, and a 1,080 by 566 landscape at the widest. Making the picture the right shape yourself means
      Instagram has nothing to crop, so you choose what stays in the frame.
    </p>

    <h2>Reel cover</h2>
    <p>
      For Reels, the Help Center recommends a cover photo of 420 by 654 pixels, a ratio of 1:1.55. The page said at the time we read it that a
      cover cannot be edited after you upload it, so it is worth checking the preview first. The tool has a preset for
      this size. Reels themselves accept shapes from 1.91:1 to 9:16 and need at least 720 pixels, but this page works with
      pictures, not video.
    </p>

    <h2>Crop, colour bars or blur</h2>
    <p>
      When your picture is not the shape of the frame, there are three honest choices. Crop fills the frame and cuts
      off the overflow, and you pick whether to keep the centre, the top, the bottom, the left or the right. Colour bars
      keep the whole picture and fill the empty space with white, black or a colour you choose. The blurred background
      keeps the whole picture too, and fills the space with an enlarged, softened copy of the same picture, which
      suits photos with a lot of colour.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use crop for portraits where the subject is in the middle, and set the focus if they are not.</li>
      <li>Use colour bars for graphics, text or screenshots that must not be cut.</li>
      <li>Use the blurred background for a wide photo you want to show whole in a square post.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What this page does not cover</h2>
    <p>
      The Instagram Help Center pages we read, on photos and Reels, do not state a pixel size for Stories or a
      profile picture, so those sizes are not offered here. Guides on other websites give numbers for them, but
      they are not from Instagram, and platform sizes change. If you need one, check Instagram's current guidance in the
      app.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping the file small</h2>
    <p>
      The result is a JPEG saved at good quality. If you also need the file under a number of kilobytes, type it in the
      size limit box and the tool lowers the quality to try to fit it, and says if it could not. The <a href="/">main resizer</a> can do that for any
      picture, and you can turn a sideways photo with the <a href="/rotate-image/">rotator</a> first.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about Instagram image sizes</h2>
    <h3>Why does Instagram crop my photo?</h3>
    <p>
      The Help Center says a photo outside the supported shape range is cropped to a supported one. Fitting it yourself
      with crop, bars or blur lets you decide what is kept.
    </p>
    <h3>What width should I use?</h3>
    <p>
      Instagram says it shares photos at up to 1,080 pixels wide. A photo wider than that is scaled down, so more pixels
      do not help.
    </p>
    <h3>Will a small photo look sharp?</h3>
    <p>
      Not always. A photo smaller than the size you pick is enlarged, which adds no detail, and the tool tells you when
      that happens.
    </p>
    <h3>Where do the sizes come from, and how current are they?</h3>
    <p>
      Each size names its source and the date we last read it, and links to our sizes guide, which lists the official Instagram Help Center page. Platforms
      change their rules, so use that date to judge how fresh a number is.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my photo uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The fitting happens in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details. Other
      platforms are covered by the <a href="/linkedin-image-resizer/">LinkedIn</a> and{' '}
      <a href="/youtube-thumbnail-resizer/">YouTube thumbnail</a> pages.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/instagram-image-resizer/" />
</Base>
